<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For each situation, choose the method that fits the information given.</p><p>(a) An aerial photograph of a forest with a grid of squares printed over it. No measurements were made on the ground.</p><div>[ none of these: the area cannot be estimated | counting squares | the trapezoidal rule | the mid-ordinate rule ] </div><p>(b) The depth of an irrigation canal 3.5 m wide, measured at both edges and every 0.5 m across.</p><div>[ none of these: the area cannot be estimated | counting squares | the trapezoidal rule | the mid-ordinate rule ] </div><p>(c) The width of a tea plot measured only at 10 m, 30 m and 50 m along a straight track 60 m long.</p><div>[ none of these: the area cannot be estimated | counting squares | the trapezoidal rule | the mid-ordinate rule ] </div><p>(d) The width of a school garden measured at both ends and every 4 m along a straight path 20 m long.</p><div>[ none of these: the area cannot be estimated | counting squares | the trapezoidal rule | the mid-ordinate rule ]  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Counting squares needs only an outline; the trapezoidal rule needs heights at the edges of the strips; the mid-ordinate rule needs heights at the middles.</p><p>(a) counting squares: there is only an outline on a grid, with no measurements.</p><p>(b) the trapezoidal rule: the measurements are at the edges of equal strips, including both ends.</p><p>(c) the mid-ordinate rule: the measurements are at the middles of equal strips, with none at the edges.</p><p>(d) the trapezoidal rule: the measurements are at the edges of equal strips, including both ends.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>counting squares</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the trapezoidal rule</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the mid-ordinate rule</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the trapezoidal rule</p></answer>
</stack-static>